    "created_at": "2015-11-25T22:45:34Z",
    "updated_at": "2015-11-25T22:50:03Z",
    "body": "> So I'm still worried that given that some browsers don't support bfcache at all, pages start to rely on \n> 'manual' meaning 'when going back to the previous page, page will be at 0,0' always.\n\nThe spec does not guarantee that the page will be at `(0,0)`. In particular this is the case for same-page navigations where document's body is never scrolled and retains its last scroll position. Note that the majority of this API usage is expected to be in SPA apps where they are dealing with same-document navigation and thus cannot assume `(0,0)` in any browser. So I think that developers are not going to assume the scroll position to be `(0,0)` regardless of bf-cache case. Anyone that want to assume scroll position to be `(0,0)` will simply do a `window.scrollTo(0,0)` instead of trying to reason about all various navigation types and bf-cache cases. I think this can be made more clear in the appropriate developer documentation e.g., MDN.\n\n> So 'manual' becomes like \"page may or may not be at 0,0 after pageshow\" and 'auto' is 'page will be  at the same position as when it was moved to session history'.\n> That 'may or may not' is rather unexpected for 'manual', I'd expect something called 'auto' to give\n> such uncertainty.\n\nThe 'scrollRestoration' attribute only determines the agent responsible for restoring scroll position. It does not guarantee any particular final scroll position.\n`scrollRestoration: auto`: user agent may or may not restore scroll position\n`scrollRestoration: manual`: user agent will not restore scroll position\n\nNeither value guarantees any particular scroll position (e.g., ``(0,0)`) when considering all navigation types (same doc, cross doc).\n",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "pin": null,
    "reactions": {
